(3730273, 515924, 72317, '<td valign="top">I like it better how it is..<br/>But maybe that''s just me.</td>', 'I like it better how it is..But maybe that''s just me.', '2007-06-02 22:30:08.000000', NULL, 0),
(3730378, 512282, 10402, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Good action shot. The players frozen in mid-air give a strong feeling of motion that nicely captures the excitement of the game. I really love the strong diagonal line that starts at 41''s right foot, goes up the wrinkles of his shirt and through his left arm, then through the stripe on 15''s shirt and up to the ball. You couldn''t have gotten that better if you had been able to pose them!<br/> <br/>I''m ambivalent about the selective desaturation here. Certainly having the ball in color is effective; it''s the focal point here and that draws even more attention to it. And the color in 15''s stripe helps it''s role in the composition I described above. But the color in 41''s shirt draws undue attention to itself and detracts from the composition slightly. However, I think having one player''s uniform in color and not the other would look really strange. So this photo isn''t really ideal for selective desaturation, but you''ve probably made the best choice here.<br/> <br/>A major weakness in the composition is that the ball crowds the edge of the frame. Since it''s the focal point, it needs "room to breathe". The photo would also be much better if the ball was in focus.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Good action shot. The players frozen in mid-air give a strong feeling of motion that nicely captures the excitement of the game. I really love the strong diagonal line that starts at 41''s right foot, goes up the wrinkles of his shirt and through his left arm, then through the stripe on 15''s shirt and up to the ball. You couldn''t have gotten that better if you had been able to pose them! I''m ambivalent about the selective desaturation here. Certainly having the ball in color is effective; it''s the focal point here and that draws even more attention to it. And the color in 15''s stripe helps it''s role in the composition I described above. But the color in 41''s shirt draws undue attention to itself and detracts from the composition slightly. However, I think having one player''s uniform in color and not the other would look really strange. So this photo isn''t really ideal for selective desaturation, but you''ve probably made the best choice here. A major weakness in the composition is that the ball crowds the edge of the frame. Since it''s the focal point, it needs "room to breathe". The photo would also be much better if the ball was in focus.', '2007-06-02 23:22:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(3730603, 516411, 64994, '<td valign="top">Jon, great shot!! I am really suprised this didn''t do better! tough challenge I know but still I would have expected this to be much closer to if not a 6. Very nice city scene and great choice for desat, great work!!</td>', 'Jon, great shot!! I am really suprised this didn''t do better! tough challenge I know but still I would have expected this to be much closer to if not a 6. Very nice city scene and great choice for desat, great work!!', '2007-06-03 00:36:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(3730654, 515132, 10402, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>My first impression looking at this photo was that the red flower didn''t really belong. So I wasn''t surprised to see your comment that you placed it there. It looks unnatural. But that isn''t really important; photos, even flower photos, don''t necessarily have to look natural. It depends on what message the photographer wants to get across. And this photo <i>is</i> attractive. The red flower does stand out, and makes the viewer wonder why it''s there. There is no correct answer; the viewer gets to decide for himself. That''s a hallmark of good art.<br/> <br/>Now I want to disagree with Steve''s critique a bit! Don''t be frustrated by our disagreement; this is all subjective, and that''s what makes art interesting!<br/> <br/>I like the highly saturated red. The color amidst the gray already draws attention to it, and the intensity, well, intensifies this. I think it works very well. And I also like the reddish reflection; it helps the red flower blend in. Cloning it out makes the red flower look even more unnatural. (Of course, if that''s your goal, go for it!) But there is a very light bluish hue on the petals behind and above the red flower that looks out of place; I do suggest removing it. (I would recommend using a layer mask on a hue/saturation layer rather than cloning to do this.)<br/> <br/>While the soft focus is certainly fitting, these flowers have such a wonderful texture that I would prefer it sharper. A bit of sharpening to bring out the details would really enhance this. <br/> <br/>But I do agree with Steve''s recommendation to crop off the left side. That would better balance the composition.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  My first impression looking at this photo was that the red flower didn''t really belong. So I wasn''t surprised to see your comment that you placed it there. It looks unnatural. But that isn''t really important; photos, even flower photos, don''t necessarily have to look natural. It depends on what message the photographer wants to get across. And this photo is attractive. The red flower does stand out, and makes the viewer wonder why it''s there. There is no correct answer; the viewer gets to decide for himself. That''s a hallmark of good art. Now I want to disagree with Steve''s critique a bit! Don''t be frustrated by our disagreement; this is all subjective, and that''s what makes art interesting! I like the highly saturated red. The color amidst the gray already draws attention to it, and the intensity, well, intensifies this. I think it works very well. And I also like the reddish reflection; it helps the red flower blend in. Cloning it out makes the red flower look even more unnatural. (Of course, if that''s your goal, go for it!) But there is a very light bluish hue on the petals behind and above the red flower that looks out of place; I do suggest removing it. (I would recommend using a layer mask on a hue/saturation layer rather than cloning to do this.) While the soft focus is certainly fitting, these flowers have such a wonderful texture that I would prefer it sharper. A bit of sharpening to bring out the details would really enhance this.  But I do agree with Steve''s recommendation to crop off the left side. That would better balance the composition.', '2007-06-03 00:51:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(3731024, 513042, 69325, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by roz:</b></div><hr/><i> love this photograph and love the choice of selective desat .. this image has a great feel to it .. i reckon it should''v done better, but i say that ALL THE TIME!!! ..:) </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>i wish you were the only one that voted ;)</td>', 'Originally posted by roz: love this photograph and love the choice of selective desat .. this image has a great feel to it .. i reckon it should''v done better, but i say that ALL THE TIME!!! ..:)   i wish you were the only one that voted ;)', '2007-06-03 04:59:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(3731668, 516588, 67841, '<td valign="top">Bravo, Hannah!  <br/> <br/>This was one of my favs from the challenge - I''m glad it finished well, but it should have hit the top 5, IMHO...  <br/> <br/>Your lighting and composition was fantastic &amp; your pp just fantastic!</td>', 'Bravo, Hannah!   This was one of my favs from the challenge - I''m glad it finished well, but it should have hit the top 5, IMHO...   Your lighting and composition was fantastic & your pp just fantastic!', '2007-06-03 11:53:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(3732417, 516326, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>Great leading lines in this shot. The line of the wall takes the viewer into the frame as does the ceiling tiles. <br/> <br/>Lighting is pretty good. Previous commenters said not to use a flash. For me the shadows actually work here as it lends to an overall dark feeling that helps create a mood. I like that the hallway behind her becomes quite dark and is symbolic of the child''s fears. The light in the doorway behind her help separate her from the background nicely.<br/> <br/>Exposure is pretty good. A larger aperture would have helped separate the subject from the background more but I kinda like the detail in the background anyways for this shot. <br/> <br/>The challenge: As mentioned previously, some voters might have felt that this was a staged shot. In the two second view of some voters, they have also not understood why the bear was desaturated. The average score given by commenters shows that the viewer needs to look at this shot for a while to really "get it"<br/> <br/>Well done!</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club! Great leading lines in this shot. The line of the wall takes the viewer into the frame as does the ceiling tiles.  Lighting is pretty good. Previous commenters said not to use a flash. For me the shadows actually work here as it lends to an overall dark feeling that helps create a mood. I like that the hallway behind her becomes quite dark and is symbolic of the child''s fears. The light in the doorway behind her help separate her from the background nicely. Exposure is pretty good. A larger aperture would have helped separate the subject from the background more but I kinda like the detail in the background anyways for this shot.  The challenge: As mentioned previously, some voters might have felt that this was a staged shot. In the two second view of some voters, they have also not understood why the bear was desaturated. The average score given by commenters shows that the viewer needs to look at this shot for a while to really "get it" Well done!', '2007-06-03 14:48:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(3732438, 513187, 66515, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club!<br/> <br/>As previous commenters have indicated, desaturating the pump just makes it look like a pump that is painted grey. You mentioned its a blue pump so perhaps desaturating the background and then bumping up the saturation on the pump itself would be quite dramatic. Perhaps you can show us an outake like this?<br/> <br/>That aside, the sharpness is great in this shot. The background is faded nicely and it well separated from the subject.<br/> <br/>Good exposure with nice tonal range throughout. Some of the shadows are a little harsh in the pump but they create very interesting lines.<br/> <br/>Composition is excellent and I like how the handle helps balance the scene <br/> <br/>Overall, this is a really strong shot that unfortunately got slammed by the voters because it likely received a lot of DNMC votes.</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club! As previous commenters have indicated, desaturating the pump just makes it look like a pump that is painted grey. You mentioned its a blue pump so perhaps desaturating the background and then bumping up the saturation on the pump itself would be quite dramatic. Perhaps you can show us an outake like this? That aside, the sharpness is great in this shot. The background is faded nicely and it well separated from the subject. Good exposure with nice tonal range throughout. Some of the shadows are a little harsh in the pump but they create very interesting lines. Composition is excellent and I like how the handle helps balance the scene  Overall, this is a really strong shot that unfortunately got slammed by the voters because it likely received a lot of DNMC votes.', '2007-06-03 14:55:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(3732570, 516973, 46091, '<td valign="top">i dont mean to bash either, but i''ve seen pretty much this same exact picture a while ago.</td>', 'i dont mean to bash either, but i''ve seen pretty much this same exact picture a while ago.', '2007-06-03 16:12:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(3732799, 515854, 4162, '<td valign="top">Hi from the critique club:)<br/> <br/>firstly- great shot and congratulations on a good finish-this was a tough one and you did very well.<br/> <br/>I think that you have used the effect here with meaning and in a way that adds strength to the picture.  The colour really highlights the main parts of the picture and they really stand out against the background.. I think that the title works well with this and it makes people appreciate the kinds of jobs and dangeer that these people do...so yes, your image makes people think.<br/> <br/>I do wish that the rest of the rope was colourized..but as you said this is down to taste...for me it would have worked better with colour.<br/> <br/>Great toning, great sharpeness.....Great shot!!  Good Job. Natasha</td>', 'Hi from the critique club:) firstly- great shot and congratulations on a good finish-this was a tough one and you did very well. I think that you have used the effect here with meaning and in a way that adds strength to the picture.  The colour really highlights the main parts of the picture and they really stand out against the background.. I think that the title works well with this and it makes people appreciate the kinds of jobs and dangeer that these people do...so yes, your image makes people think. I do wish that the rest of the rope was colourized..but as you said this is down to taste...for me it would have worked better with colour. Great toning, great sharpeness.....Great shot!!  Good Job. Natasha', '2007-06-03 17:15:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(3732818, 516269, 4162, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club:)<br/> <br/>Firstly, the composition of this is great- the 3 main subjects are spot on in focus and everything is very clean and sharp..great.<br/> <br/>The use of colour is very effective here, the brass is beautiful and very rich and this really stands out against the monochrome-the saturation of the monochrome is spot on... I think I agree with JuliBoc- I would have prefered this if just the middle trumpet was coloured, I think this would have been more dramatic and add more meaning.<br/> <br/>Great shot and excellet use of the technique.<br/> <br/>Natasha</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club:) Firstly, the composition of this is great- the 3 main subjects are spot on in focus and everything is very clean and sharp..great. The use of colour is very effective here, the brass is beautiful and very rich and this really stands out against the monochrome-the saturation of the monochrome is spot on... I think I agree with JuliBoc- I would have prefered this if just the middle trumpet was coloured, I think this would have been more dramatic and add more meaning. Great shot and excellet use of the technique. Natasha', '2007-06-03 17:21:12.000000', NULL, 0),
